# React Component Patterns

## Page Components Are Thin Orchestrators

Pages live in `src/pages/`. Their only jobs are: call hooks, derive minimal local state, and pass everything down to presentational children. They must not contain significant JSX logic, styling blocks, or UI sub-components inline.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD — page doing too much
export function Letters() {
  const [letters, setLetters] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => { /* inline fetch */ }, []);
  return (
    <div>
      <input onChange={...} />
      <table>{letters.map(l => <tr>...</tr>)}</table>
    </div>
  );
}

// ✅ GOOD — page delegates to focused children
export function Letters() {
  const { letters, loading, error, refetch } = useLetters(sortBy, sortOrder);
  const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState('created_at');
  return <LetterList letters={letters} loading={loading} onSort={setSortBy} />;
}
```

## Sub-Components Do One Thing

UI components live in `src/components/` organized by domain (e.g. `Letters/`, `Templates/`, `Documents/`, `CreateLetter/`). Shared primitives live in `src/components/ui/`. Each component should have a single, named responsibility.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD — catch-all component
function LetterCard({ letter, onEdit, onDelete, onDownload, showBadge, isLoading }) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — focused components composed together
function LetterCard({ letter }: { letter: Letter }) { ... }
function LetterActions({ onEdit, onDelete, onDownload }: LetterActionsProps) { ... }
function LetterStatusBadge({ status }: { status: string }) { ... }
```

## Co-location Rule

If a sub-component is used by only one page, it may be co-located in `src/components/<Domain>/` alongside other domain components. If it is used in two or more places, it belongs in `src/components/ui/` or a shared domain folder.

